+"""The verify / DSpark cost table the depth policy prices with (``depth.Costs``), measured at boot on real text and
+cached per (image, knobs, shape) (GLM ``spark/calib.py`` ``real`` / ``cached``, patches/0070 / 0140).
+
+Measurement (both ranks in lockstep, ``measure``): rank 0's calibration text (GLM's ``calib.TEXT`` in the
+checkpoint's tokens, shared) is prefilled in one slot, 15 tokens are decoded serially (window + commit), the slot is
+prefilled again to the same point, and every verify window of 1 to 16 rows (the pending token + the model's own
+continuation: exactly what a fully accepted round verifies there) is timed (``method``, below); then one DSpark pass
+for one slot. Each number is the slower rank's (an int exchange of microseconds, the maximum), so both ranks hold the
+same table: since VERSION 4 every row's own measured value, made non-decreasing (``monotone``).
+
+The table's shape (G15, ``VERSION`` 4). Until VERSION 3 only the 1-row entry was raw: 2-8 rows sat on a fitted line
+and 9-16 on a second line from the 8-row value (GLM ``window_costs``). The real curve is not two lines. G14's 51 boots
+(5 passes each, ``results/G14-20261003``) give, as table minus the measured window: +1.3 ms at 2 rows, -0.8 at 5, +1.1
+to +1.3 at 7-8, -2.3 to -2.6 at 9-10 and 13-14 (the steps at 9 and 13 rows), in every config. The 2-row entry read
+~1.1-1.4 ms high (~4%), so the depth policy shied away from 2-row rounds. The windows behind those lines were timed
+anyway (rows 1-16, every boot). Now each row keeps its own value; the timed runs per row, applied to one pass against
+the median of the others, are within 0.04-0.26 ms mean abs at 1-8 rows and 0.3-0.6 at 9-16.
+
+The method. Until G13 each window ran once to warm up and then 3 timed runs, and the fastest was kept. The first
+windows timed after a prefill run slow: G14a measured 24-25.9 ms against 23.1-23.5 settled, and G13 read 31.5 ms in
+one boot whose serving windows were normal. The depth policy, priced from that, never chose a 1-row round (prose
+-3.4%). G14 (VERSION 3) added 2 warm-ups, the median of 5 and rows 1-2 rechecked after the sweep. Two of its 39 boots
+still read 0.58 / 0.71 ms high at 1 row. G14's samples show why: a light window run after heavier work (the prefill,
+the 16-row and wide windows before the recheck) reads high in a slow mode (+0.6-0.9 ms, a median +0.83) for ~5-6
+windows. Of the timed runs after 2 warm-ups, the 1st was high 58-67% of the time, the 2nd 38-40%, the 3rd 26-30% and
+the 4th-5th 18-22%; the settled rate stays ~18%. That is decay after load, not noise: it is the same in the passes run
+long after boot. With the timed runs high ~1 time in 3, the median of 5 is high ~20% of the time and both blocks
+~4% (2 of 39 seen). A cold L2 would cost 1 window, not 5-6; the decay looks like the GPU clock or power state
+recovering after heavy work (not measured directly: no clock trace in G14). Now (G15):
+
+- rows 1 to ``TF_DSV41_CALIB_CYCLE`` (4) are timed as interleaved cycles (1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, ...):
+  ``TF_DSV41_CALIB_WARM`` (3) discarded cycles (12 light windows, past the decay), then ``TF_DSV41_CALIB_REPS`` (5)
+  timed cycles, and each row's ``TF_DSV41_CALIB_STAT`` (``median``; ``min`` = G13's rule). Interleaved, a slow
+  stretch lands on every row alike rather than on one row's block. With the timed runs high ~18% of the time, the
+  median of 5 is high ~4% and both blocks ~0.2%;
+- rows 1 to ``TF_DSV41_CALIB_RECHECK`` (4) are timed again the same way after the whole sweep. Each row keeps the
+  lower of the two blocks' statistics, so one transient at the start (or the end) cannot set it;
+- rows 5-8: ``WARM`` warm-ups and ``REPS`` timed runs a row; rows 9-16: 1 warm-up and ``TF_DSV41_CALIB_DEEP_REPS``
+  (5) timed runs; the wide buckets and the DSpark pass: 1 warm-up and 3 timed runs (interpolation points, or less
+  weight).
+
+Boot cost (G14's off windows): ~6.7 s of windows against VERSION 3's ~4.7 s (+~2 s: rows 9-16's two more runs
++1.1 s, rows 1-4 cycled and rechecked +0.9 s).
+``TF_DSV41_CALIB_SHAPE=fit`` brings back VERSION 3 whole: its defaults (warm 2, recheck 2, no cycle, deep 3) and its
+fitted table. Explicit knobs still override, so ``SHAPE=fit REPS=3 WARM=1 STAT=min RECHECK=0`` is G13's. These knobs
+are in ``KNOBS``: tables measured by different methods are cached apart.
+
+Shared windows (the joint depth, ``joint.py``, prices a round by its TOTAL rows): with more slots, every slot is
+prefilled with its own cut of the text and decodes its own continuation, then the wide row-graph buckets (``WIDE``:
+24, 32, 48, 64 rows; at most 16 rows a slot, so ceil(R / 16) slots) are timed as one window each, and an 8-row window
+split 4 + 4 over two slots against the 8 rows of one slot gives ``Costs.slot`` (a further slot's own overhead). The
+table runs to the widest bucket measured (rows 17 .. 64 interpolated between the buckets, the slot overhead taken
+out); ``Costs.rows_ms`` extends it past that on its last line.
+
+Cache (``TF_DSV41_CALIB``): ``cached`` (default) reads the table stored for this key, else measures and stores it;
+``real`` always measures (and refreshes the entry); ``default`` uses ``depth.default_costs`` (no measurement). The
+key: the code digest of the image (``engine.code_digest``), the knobs that change a window's time (``KNOBS``) and
+the shape (slots, context, ranks, the GPU name). Files: ``TF_DSV41_CALIB_DIR`` (default
+~/.cache/tensorfold/dsv41-calib), one JSON per key. Rank 0 reads the entry and shares it.
